  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nUpper level ridging over northern Mexico will continue through \nFriday before a shortwave trough will move across the southern \nPlains on Saturday.  This will weaken the ridge and push it further \nsouth into Mexico for the rest of the weekend.  Warm and humid \nconditions with southeasterly surface flow will continue for \nSaturday through Monday resulting in persistent high temperatures in \nthe mid 90s. This will lead to some low chances (10-20%) for \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms each afternoon.\n\nThe pattern will shift and become much murkier starting Monday \nnight. Confidence is increasing on an upper-level trough moving out \nof the Pacific Northwest by Tuesday.  This trough should push a \nfairly strong and Autumn-like surface cold front southward through \nthe central Plains and into north Texas through Wednesday.  This \ncould potentially bring more seasonal temperatures into west central \nTexas for mid-week. The ensemble spread of temperatures ranges from \nhighs in the mid 70s to around 90 degrees with a mean in the low \n80s.  The forecast will hinge on the strength of this front and how \nfar south this feature makes it into Texas.  For now, high \ntemperatures were kept in the low to mid 90s for Tuesday and in the \nmid 80s for Wednesday.  The other uncertainty is rainfall chances \nand timing, which will depend heavily on the path of Hurricane Polo \nas it moves close to Baja California in Mexico. Currently, global \nmodels diverge on the path of Polo, but more than half of ensemble \nmembers show the storm moving into northwest Mexico on Tuesday.  If \nthis scenario were to occur Polo's remnants would merge with the \naforementioned trough and cold front which will increase rain \nchances more significantly due to the increase in moisture. For now, \ngiven the high uncertainty, PoPs were kept at low to medium (30-50%) \nfor Tuesday and Wednesday.",
